WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.540
What you're looking at is a clam's foot.

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:06.450
That's right, clams have a foot.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:09.870
And some species can
extend it several inches.

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:12.312
But as it turns out, a foot is just one

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:14.228
of the many bizarre features

00:00:14.228 --> 00:00:16.511
you'll find inside a clam.

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:20.910
Like oysters and mussels,
clams are bivalves,

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:23.490
a kind of mollusk that's
encased in a shell

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:26.440
made of two valves, or hinging parts.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:29.090
And that shell comes
in all different sizes.

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:30.940
There are small clams, like these,

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:32.650
which are often used for cooking.

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:35.040
And then there are giant clams,

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.700
which can grow more than a meter long

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:41.140
and weigh as much as 250 kilograms.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:42.972
But no matter their size, clams have

00:00:42.972 --> 00:00:46.290
some truly bizarre stuff going on inside.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:49.584
And to get a closer
look, we went clamming.

00:00:49.584 --> 00:00:51.780
Benji Jones: Oh, God. [laughing]

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:54.210
Narrator: That's marine
biologist Soren Dahl,

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.746
who took us clamming on Long Island.

00:00:57.704 --> 00:00:59.700
At first, we didn't have much luck.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.410
Benji: I got one, but
it looks like it's dead.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:04.810
Soren Dahl: Old shell.

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:06.290
Benji: Oh, I got a crab.

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:07.850
Narrator: Then we
stumbled upon what's known

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:09.140
as a honey hole.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:12.340
It's basically a clammer's
code word for jackpot.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:14.260
Benji: Hey! I got two! [laughing]

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:15.710
Soren: All right!

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:17.093
Narrator: And in the end, we found about

00:01:17.093 --> 00:01:18.820
a dozen hard clams.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:22.223
Benji: This right here
is my recently collected,

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:24.448
my recently collected clams,

00:01:24.448 --> 00:01:27.360
and I have a lot of them.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:28.440
Narrator: Now, to see what's inside,

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:31.470
you can't exactly pry one
open with your bare hands.

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:33.950
And that's thanks to these two muscles.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:37.010
They run from the inside
of one shell to the other,

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:38.820
and when clams sense a threat

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:42.100
or find themselves out
of water, they contract,

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.511
causing the clam to, you know,

00:01:44.511 --> 00:01:45.630
[snapping]

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:46.730
clam up.

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:48.163
So, to get one open,

00:01:48.163 --> 00:01:50.470
you need to cut through those muscles.

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:52.036
Soren: Now, you have your,

00:01:52.036 --> 00:01:53.670
this is your hard-shell clam.

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:55.843
Benji: Both of, so those
two muscles together

00:01:55.843 --> 00:01:58.440
create a muscle that
goes from here to here?

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:01.950
Soren: Yep, this goes from
this side to that side.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:04.410
Narrator: And what
about that strange foot?

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:06.620
Soren: This is the foot, right here.

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:09.820
Narrator: So, it might not
be as impressive as this,

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:13.220
but that's just because
here the foot is retracted.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:16.300
Soren: And they'll extend
this out of the clam,

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:18.840
and they can push
themselves along the bottom

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.910
and use it to dig a hole

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:23.380
to help bring themselves
into, like, a burrow.

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:25.990
Narrator: And some clams are particularly

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:28.340
dexterous with their foot.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:29.890
The razor clam, for example,

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:33.450
can bury itself 70
centimeters underground.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:35.900
It uses rapid movements of its foot

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:38.200
to fluidize the ground around it,

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.810
essentially turning it into quicksand.

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:43.650
But if you think clam feet are bizarre,

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:46.220
check out this thing: the siphon.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:48.930
Siphons are essentially
two connected straws

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:51.430
that clams stick out of their shells.

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:52.630
One pulls in water,

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:55.250
which contains food particles and oxygen,

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:57.330
and the other expels waste.

00:02:57.330 --> 00:02:59.050
And none is more impressive

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:01.950
than the one belonging
to the geoduck clam.

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:05.580
It's so big the clam can't
even close its shell.

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.350
While siphons allow
clams to eat and breathe,

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:11.790
some clams have another,
more advanced tactic

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:13.410
for getting nutrients.

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:15.730
Instead of just sucking
food out of the water,

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:18.330
giant clams farm it themselves.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:21.320
Like coral, they have a
symbiotic relationship

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:22.940
with microalgae.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:25.440
The clams provide algae
with a place to live

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.520
and photosynthesize, and in exchange,

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:32.220
algae gives the clam
byproducts of photosynthesis,

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:35.340
such as sugars, which
enables the giant clams

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:37.550
to grow, you know, giant.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.210
Now, on rare occasions,

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:42.040
there's something else you
might find inside a clam:

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:43.150
a pearl.

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:44.690
That's right, oysters aren't

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:46.820
the only pearl producers out there.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:49.610
Clams, too, will form
these shimmering clumps

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:52.480
to trap irritants that enter their shells.

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:54.488
In fact, a fisherman once found

00:03:54.488 --> 00:03:56.896
a 34-kilogram pearl

00:03:56.896 --> 00:03:58.890
inside a giant clam.

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:01.290
So, yeah, clams aren't just fascinating;

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:03.580
they might also be precious.

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:05.840
Plus, they actually play an important role

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:07.150
in their environment.

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:08.820
By sucking up particles to eat,

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:11.930
they function like natural water filters,

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:15.490
making marshes, lakes, and
other habitats more livable.

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:18.270
And, on top of that,
they're delicious to eat.

00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:21.203
Not to mention, really fun to collect.

